body:
At least in Firefox, this has nothing to do with the clone itself and the original node. The `<link>` sheet would be immediately available after binding to the tree if it links to the same `href` (and has the same cache characteristics). This is because of the in-memory cache (works very much like `<img>` and the spec'd list of available images).

This is something that (within the same page) has been around since ~forever in Gecko, fwiw.

If spec changes are needed, I'd much rather follow `<img>` than making up something specific to `cloneNode()`.

Here's a test-case that shows that behavior fwiw:

```html
<!doctype html>
<link id="source" rel="stylesheet" href="data:text/css,:root{color: green}">
<button onclick="cloneIt(true)">clone</button>
<button onclick="cloneIt(false)">new</button>
<script>
function cloneIt(useCloneNode) {
  let source = document.getElementById("source");
  let target;
  if (useCloneNode) {
    target = source.cloneNode(true);
  } else {
    target = document.createElement("link");
    target.rel = source.rel;
    target.href = source.href;
  }
  console.log("cloned", target, target.sheet);
  document.head.appendChild(target);
  console.log("inserted", target, target.sheet);
}
</script>
```
